/* anime-sh — the home screen's visual language.

   Three tiers of background carry the depth, not boxes. Terminal rows are
   scarce: a border costs two of them per section, and four sections of borders
   costs eight rows of content to say something a shade of background already
   says. So:

       $background   the base the screen sits on          (darkest)
       $surface      a plate that content sits on          (a step up)
       $panel        a thing lifted off that plate         (a step further)

   This is the one change that matters most. The old sheet set `Screen` to
   `$surface` — the MIDDLE tier — which left nowhere to go up: rows, rail,
   headings and background were all the same colour, and the whole screen read
   as one flat wall of text. The tiers were always in the theme; they just were
   not being used.

   Colour has one job each. `$accent` means "the keyboard is here" and appears
   nowhere else, so it is never ambiguous. Headings are structure, not
   interaction, and are set in the foreground colour instead. */

Screen {
    background: $background;
}

#search {
    margin: 1 2 0 2;
    border: tall $panel;
    background: $surface;
}

#search:focus {
    border: tall $accent;
}

#body {
    padding: 0 2;
    width: 1fr;
}

/* Region C — the context rail. Width is set from the screen width in
   `_size_rail` rather than here, because the boundary it depends on (120
   columns) is not something CSS can ask about.

   It used to be separated by a single hairline, which read as a leftover strip
   rather than a panel: a rule between two identical backgrounds says "here is a
   line", not "here is a different kind of thing". Sitting it on the surface
   tier is what makes it a region. */
#rail {
    width: 34;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $surface;
}

/* The rail is glanced at, never read, so it stays a step back from the rows —
   but muted, not invisible: it was previously dim enough to look disabled. */
#rail-body {
    color: $text-muted;
}

#sec-rail {
    color: $text;
    text-style: bold;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    padding: 0;
}

/* Section headings sit on the BASE, above the plate their rows sit on — the
   ordinary "label above a card" arrangement, and the reason the plate reads as
   a plate.

   They used to be `$accent` bold, which made the loudest thing on the screen
   the part carrying the least information: four orange headings competing with
   the rows for attention while the selected row got a thin bar. Accent now
   means one thing only, and it is not "heading". */
.section {
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
    padding: 0 1;
    text-style: bold;
    color: $text;
}

/* The plate. Padding is what turns a background change into a panel: without
   it the tint stops flush against the text and reads as a highlight rather than
   as a surface the rows are sitting on. */
ListView {
    background: $surface;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 20;
    padding: 1 1;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* Every row reserves the marker gutter, whether or not it is showing one — as
   padding, not as a border. Painting the border only on the focused row made
   that row one cell narrower than its rest, so the grid had to be cut to the
   smaller of the two or the focused row clipped, and the width flipped back and
   forth as focus moved.

   Reserving it as an always-on border was the first attempt and was worse: with
   a border on every row the focused one differed only in the *colour* of it, so
   on a monochrome terminal nothing distinguished it at all. Padding gives back
   the column without drawing anything, and the focused row swaps one cell of it
   for a border — same width, and the marker is a shape again. */
ListView > ListItem {
    padding: 0 1 0 2;
    background: $surface;
}

/* Rows are a grid: each one is exactly one line, and its cells are padded to
   fixed widths in rows.py. Pinning the height here means a title long enough to
   defeat the truncation still cannot wrap and shunt every row below it out of
   alignment. */
AnimeItem {
    height: 1;
}

AnimeItem Label {
    height: 1;
    width: 1fr;
}

/* The class is `-highlight`, one dash. Textual applies `-highlight`; this file
   once said `--highlight`, so the selection rule matched nothing and had never
   styled anything — a dead CSS selector raises no error and renders no
   complaint, it just quietly does nothing.

   Which list the keyboard is driving has to be visible. Every list keeps a
   highlighted row so you do not lose your place moving between sections, but
   only the focused one gets the accent. The unfocused ones lift to the panel
   tier instead: still findable, clearly not live.

   Colour is not carrying this alone — the left border is a shape, so the
   distinction survives a monochrome terminal. */
ListView > ListItem.-highlight {
    background: $panel;
}

ListView:focus > ListItem.-highlight {
    background: $primary 40%;
    border-left: thick $accent;
    padding: 0 1 0 1;
}

#results-empty {
    padding: 1 2;
    color: $text-muted;
}

#detail-meta {
    padding: 1 2;
    margin: 1 0;
    background: $surface;
    border: round $panel;
}

#episodes {
    padding: 1 2;
    max-height: 24;
}

/* Region C's header block: the poster, then what the row under the cursor is.
   The cover is centred over the text rather than beside it — at rail width
   there is not room for a column of each, and a poster is the one thing on this
   screen that earns being looked at rather than read. */
#rail-cover {
    height: auto;
    width: 1fr;
    align-horizontal: center;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The art is a fixed number of cells wide; the Static holding it must shrink to
   that, or it fills the rail and `align-horizontal` has nothing to centre. */
#rail-cover > Static {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

#rail-preview {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    color: $text;
}

#rail-rule {
    margin: 1 0;
    color: $panel;
}
